libpam-faciolock for Debian and Ubuntu
======================================

This package installs the PAM module and registers a pam-auth-update profile at
"Default: no". Installing it changes nothing: no PAM stack names
pam_faciolock.so until you enable the profile.

Do not hand-edit /etc/pam.d/*
-----------------------------

The profile is the supported route, and it is reversible by the same tool that
applied it:

  sudo pam-auth-update           # tick "Face authentication (Faciolock)"
  sudo pam-auth-update --remove faciolock

One profile covers sudo, su, login, sddm, the KDE lock screen and polkit,
because all six include common-auth.

Enable it on sudo first
-----------------------

The profile is all-or-nothing by construction -- it edits common-auth, which
every service includes -- so there is no way to try it on one service through
pam-auth-update. If you want a smaller first step, add two lines to
/etc/pam.d/sudo by hand, live with it for a day, and then enable the profile.
A sudo that refuses is a shell you already have; a lock screen that refuses is
not.

  sudo cp /etc/pam.d/sudo /etc/pam.d/sudo.faciolock-backup

Then, above the "@include common-auth" and "@include common-account" lines:

  auth     [success=end default=ignore]   pam_faciolock.so
  account  optional                       pam_faciolock.so

Both lines, not just the first. The auth line alone never calls the account
hook, which is where the lockout release lives -- so three refused faces would
disable face unlock for the rest of the trial and it would read as "it stopped
working" rather than "it is rate-limited, as designed".

Recovery
--------

Before making any PAM change, open a second terminal and hold a root shell in
it:

  sudo -i

That shell authenticated before the edit and does not care what /etc/pam.d says
afterwards. From it, either line undoes a by-hand trial:

  cp /etc/pam.d/sudo.faciolock-backup /etc/pam.d/sudo
  sed -i '/pam_faciolock/d' /etc/pam.d/sudo

and this undoes the profile:

  pam-auth-update --remove faciolock

If every terminal is lost as well, the recovery is a root shell from the boot
menu: hold Shift for GRUB, choose the recovery entry, and /etc/pam.d is an
ordinary directory on an ordinary filesystem. Nothing in this design can make a
machine unbootable. The worst it can do is make one service refuse to
authenticate, and every refusal path falls through to the password prompt by
construction: "default=ignore" means daemon down, socket missing, not enrolled,
rate-limited, refused, malformed reply and a panic caught at the FFI boundary
all land on the next line of the stack, which is your password.

 -- Somesh Rao <cokasomeshrao@outlook.com>
